      "updated": 1781430269027,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Preview Thumbnails (Storyboard / Scrubbing)\n- Core Principle: Fetch in batches, not per-hover.\n    > Mechanism: The server groups multiple thumbnail frames into a single\nimage grid called a \"Sprite Sheet\". The client downloads the whole sheet\nat once and uses CSS to crop and display the right frame.\n    > Result: Zero network API calls for every pixel you move your mouse.\n\n- Phase 1: Upload & Processing (Server-Side)\n    > Process: Video is transcoded, frames are extracted (e.g., every 5\nseconds), shrunk down, and packed into a grid. A metadata file (manifest)\nis created mapping timestamps to exact X,Y pixel coordinates on the\nsheet.\n\n- Phase 2: Playback Initialization (Client-Side)\n    > Process: Before the user even hovers, the player loads the map of\nwhere every thumbnail lives.\n\n- Phase 3: Scrubbing Logic (The Hover Event)\n    > Process: Mouse movements are throttled. The cursor position is\nconverted to a video timestamp, looked up in the memory map, and\ndisplayed using CSS.\n\n- Key Optimizations Layered On Top\n    > Throttling/Debouncing: Prevents firing calculation logic for every\nsingle microscopic mouse movement.\n    > Prefetching: Player silently downloads adjacent sprite sheets in\nanticipation of the user dragging further.\nMulti-resolution Storyboards: A low-density, lightweight sheet covering the\nwhole video loads instantly for fast scrubbing, while high-density sheets\nload on demand for fine details.\n    > Pure CSS Cropping: Uses standard CSS background-position to shift\nthe \"window\" over the sprite sheet, requiring zero heavy Javascript image\nrendering.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Preview Thumbnails (Storyboard / Scrubbing)\n- Core Principle: Fetch in batches, not per-hover.\n    > Mechanism: The server groups multiple thumbnail frames into a single image grid called a \"Sprite Sheet\". The client downloads the whole sheet at once and uses CSS to crop and display the right frame.\n    > Result: Zero network API calls for every pixel you move your mouse.\n\n- Phase 1: Upload & Processing (Server-Side)\n    > Process: Video is transcoded, frames are extracted (e.g., every 5 seconds), shrunk down, and packed into a grid. A metadata file (manifest) is created mapping timestamps to exact X,Y pixel coordinates on the sheet.\n\n- Phase 2: Playback Initialization (Client-Side)\n    > Process: Before the user even hovers, the player loads the map of where every thumbnail lives.\n\n- Phase 3: Scrubbing Logic (The Hover Event)\n    > Process: Mouse movements are throttled. The cursor position is converted to a video timestamp, looked up in the memory map, and displayed using CSS.\n\n- Key Optimizations Layered On Top\n    > Throttling/Debouncing: Prevents firing calculation logic for every single microscopic mouse movement.\n    > Prefetching: Player silently downloads adjacent sprite sheets in anticipation of the user dragging further.\nMulti-resolution Storyboards: A low-density, lightweight sheet covering the whole video loads instantly for fast scrubbing, while high-density sheets load on demand for fine details.\n    > Pure CSS Cropping: Uses standard CSS background-position to shift the \"window\" over the sprite sheet, requiring zero heavy Javascript image rendering.\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
