    {
      "id": "aoui3fXAGxZFf4vIU88Wf",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 259.5649859425756,
      "y": 1966.9816173390925,
      "width": 881.4882579985117,
      "height": 400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eebefa",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bQ4",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1744401587,
      "version": 389,
      "versionNonce": 1297867421,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781434065238,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Robot.txt file: simple text file placed at the root of a website that provides\ninstructions to web crawlers about which parts of the site they can or cannot crawl and\nindex, helping manage server load and control search engine access to private or\nunimportant content.\n- User-agent: *\n- Disallow: /private/\n- Crawl-delay: 10\n\n2) why not directly pushing the html to the second queue- due to size constraints hence\nwe are pushing the s3 link.  \n\n3) Kafka vs SQS, Dead Letter Queue  \n\n4) Another good point to note is always keep scaling deep dive for the last. This is\nbecause you'll have a clearer picture of the full system and can make more informed\ndecisions about where to scale.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Robot.txt file: simple text file placed at the root of a website that provides instructions to web crawlers about which parts of the site they can or cannot crawl and index, helping manage server load and control search engine access to private or unimportant content.\n- User-agent: *\n- Disallow: /private/\n- Crawl-delay: 10\n\n2) why not directly pushing the html to the second queue- due to size constraints hence we are pushing the s3 link.  \n\n3) Kafka vs SQS, Dead Letter Queue  \n\n4) Another good point to note is always keep scaling deep dive for the last. This is because you'll have a clearer picture of the full system and can make more informed decisions about where to scale.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "ituYJj5hdFK0VHwybOCfi",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1190.7043088592427,
      "y": 306.30732418433,
      "width": 979.2629278273794,
      "height": 2100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eebefa",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bQ5",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 82440605,
      "version": 788,
      "versionNonce": 1548325459,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781433993098,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) fault tolerance: Currently our crawler service is doing ton of work. we will start by breaking up\nthe work.\n- URL Fetcher:\n    > Take seed URL from frontier and request IP from DNS\n    > Fetch HTML from external server using IP\n    > Stores raw html to blob for later processing.\n- Text & Url extraction:\n    > extracts text data from html\n    > extracts url to add to the frontier queue.\n\n2) Failing to fetch URL\n- bad Solution: Manual timer\n- good solution: Kafka with manual exponential backoff\n    > kafka doest support exponential backoff so we do that part manually. We could have a\nseparate topic for failed URLs and a separate service that reads from this topic and retries the\nfetch with exponential backoff. In order to know how much to backoff, we could store the time\nthat the next fetch should occur in the message itself. When a consumer reads a message, it will\ncheck the time and if it is in the future, it will wait until that time to retry the fetch.\n- Great Solution: SQS with exponential backoff\n    > further we dont want to retry indefinitely. After certain failures we move to a dead-letter\nqueue (DLQ).\n\n3) what if web crawler goes down  \n- Kafka retains messages in a log and does not remove them even after they are read. Crawlers\ntrack their progress via offsets, which are not updated in Kafka until the URL is successfully\nfetched and processed. If a crawler fails, the next one picks up right where the last one left off,\nensuring no data is lost.  \n- With SQS, messages remain in the queue until they are explicitly deleted. A visibility timeout hides\na message from other crawlers once it's fetched. If the crawler fails before confirming successful\nprocessing, the message will automatically become visible again after the timeout expires, allowing\nanother crawler to attempt the fetch. On the other hand, once the HTML is stored in blob\nstorage, the crawler will delete the message from the queue, ensuring it is not processed again.\n\n4) Ensuring Politeness & adherence to robots.txt\n- Always check and store each domain's robots.txt before crawling.\n- Use robots.txt rules to decide:\n    > If a URL is disallowed = acknowledge and drop it.\n    > If allowed - also read any Crawl-delay value.\n- Keep a Domain Table in a metadata database to store, per domain:\n    > Parsed robots.txt rules.\n    > The last time you crawled that domain.\n- When a URL is pulled from the queue:\n    > If disallowed - ack and skip.\n    > If allowed but crawl-delay not yet passed - put it back on the queue with a delay (e.g., SQS\nDelaySeconds).\n    > If crawl-delay passed - crawl the page and update the domain's last-crawl time.\n- Enforce a global per-domain rate limit (e.g., 1 request per second) using a central store like Redis\nwith a sliding-window counter per domain.\n- If the per-domain limit is hit, wait before sending another request.\n- Add small random delays (jitter) so multiple crawlers don't all retry at exactly the same time and\ncollide on the same domain.\n\n5) Scaling a Web Crawler to 10B Pages in Under 5 Days\n- Crawling is I/O intensive; with 2MB avg page size and a 200 Gbps network-optimized AWS\ninstance, theoretical max is ~12,500 pages/sec.nRealistic utilization ~30% due to latency, DNS,\nrate limits, retries, politeness -> ~3,750 pages/sec per machine.\n    > Math: 10B pages / 3,750 pages/sec = 30.9 days on one machine; ~8 machines brings it under 5\ndays (~3.9 days).  \n\n- Per-domain rate limit (1 req/sec) is bypassed in aggregate by crawling millions of domains in parallel\nwith thousands of concurrent connections.  \n- Parser workers: auto-scale dynamically (Lambda, ECS Fargate) based on Further Processing Queue\ndepth instead of pre-sizing.  \n- DNS is a hidden bottleneck - Mercator paper found up to 70% of thread time spent on DNS\nlookups.\n    > Mitigations: DNS caching in crawlers, multiple DNS providers with round-robin, custom resolver,\nor paid rate limit increases.  \n- URL-level deduplication: check Metadata DB before enqueueing to skip already-crawled URLs.  \n- Content-level deduplication: hash fetched page content to catch identical content across\ndifferent URLs/domains.  \n    > Option 1: Store hash in Metadata DB with an index - simple and practical; modern DB indexes\nhandle scale well.  \n    > Option 2: Bloom filter (e.g., Redis + RedisBloom, BF.ADD/BF.EXISTS) - memory-efficient but\nallows false positives; often overkill.  \n- Crawler traps: pages that loop or self-link infinitely. Mitigate with a max link-hop depth (15-20),\ntracked in the URL table.  \n- Additional deep dives to consider:  \n    > Dynamic content: render JS-heavy sites with headless browsers (Puppeteer).  \n    > Monitoring: Datadog/New Relic for crawler and parser health, alerting.\n    > Large files: send HTTP HEAD first, skip if Content-Length exceeds threshold (e.g., 10MB).\n    > Continual updates: add a URL Scheduler component to recrawl based on last crawl time,\npopularity, etc.\n    > Priority crawling: multiple SQS queues by priority; Kafka needs separate topics per priority\nlevel.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) fault tolerance: Currently our crawler service is doing ton of work. we will start by breaking up the work.\n- URL Fetcher:\n    > Take seed URL from frontier and request IP from DNS\n    > Fetch HTML from external server using IP\n    > Stores raw html to blob for later processing.\n- Text & Url extraction:\n    > extracts text data from html\n    > extracts url to add to the frontier queue.\n\n2) Failing to fetch URL\n- bad Solution: Manual timer\n- good solution: Kafka with manual exponential backoff\n    > kafka doest support exponential backoff so we do that part manually. We could have a separate topic for failed URLs and a separate service that reads from this topic and retries the fetch with exponential backoff. In order to know how much to backoff, we could store the time that the next fetch should occur in the message itself. When a consumer reads a message, it will check the time and if it is in the future, it will wait until that time to retry the fetch.\n- Great Solution: SQS with exponential backoff\n    > further we dont want to retry indefinitely. After certain failures we move to a dead-letter queue (DLQ).\n\n3) what if web crawler goes down  \n- Kafka retains messages in a log and does not remove them even after they are read. Crawlers track their progress via offsets, which are not updated in Kafka until the URL is successfully fetched and processed. If a crawler fails, the next one picks up right where the last one left off, ensuring no data is lost.  \n- With SQS, messages remain in the queue until they are explicitly deleted. A visibility timeout hides a message from other crawlers once it's fetched. If the crawler fails before confirming successful processing, the message will automatically become visible again after the timeout expires, allowing another crawler to attempt the fetch. On the other hand, once the HTML is stored in blob storage, the crawler will delete the message from the queue, ensuring it is not processed again.\n\n4) Ensuring Politeness & adherence to robots.txt\n- Always check and store each domain's robots.txt before crawling.\n- Use robots.txt rules to decide:\n    > If a URL is disallowed = acknowledge and drop it.\n    > If allowed - also read any Crawl-delay value.\n- Keep a Domain Table in a metadata database to store, per domain:\n    > Parsed robots.txt rules.\n    > The last time you crawled that domain.\n- When a URL is pulled from the queue:\n    > If disallowed - ack and skip.\n    > If allowed but crawl-delay not yet passed - put it back on the queue with a delay (e.g., SQS DelaySeconds).\n    > If crawl-delay passed - crawl the page and update the domain's last-crawl time.\n- Enforce a global per-domain rate limit (e.g., 1 request per second) using a central store like Redis with a sliding-window counter per domain.\n- If the per-domain limit is hit, wait before sending another request.\n- Add small random delays (jitter) so multiple crawlers don't all retry at exactly the same time and collide on the same domain.\n\n5) Scaling a Web Crawler to 10B Pages in Under 5 Days\n- Crawling is I/O intensive; with 2MB avg page size and a 200 Gbps network-optimized AWS instance, theoretical max is ~12,500 pages/sec.nRealistic utilization ~30% due to latency, DNS, rate limits, retries, politeness -> ~3,750 pages/sec per machine.\n    > Math: 10B pages / 3,750 pages/sec = 30.9 days on one machine; ~8 machines brings it under 5 days (~3.9 days).  \n\n- Per-domain rate limit (1 req/sec) is bypassed in aggregate by crawling millions of domains in parallel with thousands of concurrent connections.  \n- Parser workers: auto-scale dynamically (Lambda, ECS Fargate) based on Further Processing Queue depth instead of pre-sizing.  \n- DNS is a hidden bottleneck - Mercator paper found up to 70% of thread time spent on DNS lookups.\n    > Mitigations: DNS caching in crawlers, multiple DNS providers with round-robin, custom resolver, or paid rate limit increases.  \n- URL-level deduplication: check Metadata DB before enqueueing to skip already-crawled URLs.  \n- Content-level deduplication: hash fetched page content to catch identical content across different URLs/domains.  \n    > Option 1: Store hash in Metadata DB with an index - simple and practical; modern DB indexes handle scale well.  \n    > Option 2: Bloom filter (e.g., Redis + RedisBloom, BF.ADD/BF.EXISTS) - memory-efficient but allows false positives; often overkill.  \n- Crawler traps: pages that loop or self-link infinitely. Mitigate with a max link-hop depth (15-20), tracked in the URL table.  \n- Additional deep dives to consider:  \n    > Dynamic content: render JS-heavy sites with headless browsers (Puppeteer).  \n    > Monitoring: Datadog/New Relic for crawler and parser health, alerting.\n    > Large files: send HTTP HEAD first, skip if Content-Length exceeds threshold (e.g., 10MB).\n    > Continual updates: add a URL Scheduler component to recrawl based on last crawl time, popularity, etc.\n    > Priority crawling: multiple SQS queues by priority; Kafka needs separate topics per priority level.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
